AB  - Large-scale pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs), such as BiomedCLIP, have demonstrated promising potential for biological image classification. However, effectively adapting these large pre-trained VLMs to specialized diagnostic tasks remains challenging due to the scarcity of annotated datasets and the prohibitive computational costs associated with full-model fine-tuning. Prompt engineering, which aims to rapidly adapt pre-trained models on small-scale, task-specific datasets, has emerged as a widely adopted alternative. Nevertheless, current prompt engineering methodologies lack standardization. To address this limitation, this work proposes a novel diagnostic axis framework. It standardizes prompts to systematically capture clinically relevant features while accounting for model sensitivity to stylistic prompt variations. This approach enhances both task adaptation and prompt interpretability for downstream diagnostic applications. On the COVID-19 Radiography Database, the model fine-tuned under the proposed framework achieved a 20.33% improvement in Accuracy compared to the baseline model. Experimental results demonstrate that models fine-tuned using diagnostic axes significantly outperformed zero-shot baseline models across most evaluation metrics on a COVID-19 screening task. Despite these promising results, this study has several limitations, such as insufficient exploration of interactions among different prompt styles and limited support for complex reasoning tasks. Future research could further incorporate potential interactions and extend support for multi-step reasoning to improve the generalizability and practical utility of the method.
